    {
      "id": 17,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        420,
        700
      ],
      "size": [
        430,
        240
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 4,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "Why cfg is 1.0",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "This is a DISTILLED checkpoint, trained to run without classifier-free guidance,\nso cfg must stay at 1.0.\n\nConsequence: with no unconditional pass the negative prompt does NOTHING. It is\nwired for structure and for the day a non-distilled checkpoint goes in. Do not try\nto fix motion by editing it.\n\nRaising cfg above 1.0 on a distilled model degrades the image, it does not add\ncontrol."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 18,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        800,
        900
      ],
      "size": [
        430,
        300
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 5,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "Length limit at this resolution",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "1280x704 (\"720p\"; 1280x720 is illegal, both axes must be multiples of 32).\nMeasured on a 4090 with this graph:\n\n  345 frames = 13.8 s   CLEAN   <- this default\n  433 frames = 17.3 s   melts into soft blobs\n  521 frames = 20.8 s   pure black\n\nLength must be 8n+1. Past the limit the job still reports success and writes a\nvalid file, so ALWAYS check the output. See docs/ltx-clip-length-limits.md and\ntools/inspect_clip.sh.\n\nOther resolutions have their own limits and they are not predicted by any formula.\n768x512 holds to 817 frames (32.7 s). Measure before trusting."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
